//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   .\SimpleSal\ss_configuration.start                   How does memory learn what it remembers?
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   FSMs are comprised of events that write and read memory to remember the state(s) they are in.
   It is often useful that FSM variables have a value when the FSM is granted Agency in Init State.

   Memory that can be written and read will usually take the form of classic RAM memory locations
   accessed through variable names in a higher-level language.  Machine language instructions only
   contain the address of the memory and an operation to be performed, such as "write 0 to memory[x]".
   Machine language only is possible while the processor is powered and executing instructions;
   the first opportunity to write values to memory locations only occurs when a processor powers on.

   The machine instructions that execute when the processor begins executing instructions may
   contain algorithms to zero memory locations (higher-level language variables with an initial
   value of 0) or copy memory values from the persistent stored copy to RAM (higher-level variables
   with an initializer that is non-0).  The higher-level language software performs this operation,
   affected by choices in the build operation that indicate the method chosen from those possible.

   The mesa, the App architecture and design, the electronic devices involved, the use of low power
   or sleep modes, everything about the build: these all affect the choice from methods possible.

   ssTEA simply shows an example of a simple bifurcation into "when the image is written into
   persistent memory such as Flash" as opposed to "when the machine instructions are executed".
   A common model is "Flash-based machine instructions copy the App image to RAM and execute it".

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
